+The critical locus of the polynomial $F|_{\mathcal T_d}$ is algebraic.
+It has finitely many irreducible components, and $F$ is constant on each:
+on the smooth dense locus of such a component its differential vanishes.
+Hence $K_0(F)$ is finite.
+
+It remains to treat $K_\infty(F)$.  Fix $N$ from Standard
+input~\ref{std:asymptotic}, and put
+\[
+ \Sigma_r=\widetilde\Sigma_{R_0}\cap\{\rho=r\}.
+\]
+Apply Hardt triviality from Standard input~\ref{std:sa} to the radius map
+on $\widetilde\Sigma_{R_0}$ and increase $R_0$ past the last exceptional
+radius.  The unbounded tail then has finitely many connected components
+$E_1,\ldots,E_k$, each meeting every sphere $\{\rho=r\}$, $r\ge R_0$, in
+a connected component $L_{j,r}$ of $\Sigma_r$.
+
+For each $j$, semialgebraic selection and finite decomposition give a
+$C^1$ semialgebraic section
+\[
+ \gamma_j:[R_0,\infty)\to E_j,
+ \qquad \rho(\gamma_j(r))=r.
+\]
+After increasing $R_0$ again, $\|\gamma_j'(r)\|\le2$.  Indeed,
+$u_j(r)=\gamma_j(r)/r$ is a bounded semialgebraic curve on the unit
+sphere.  By semialgebraic monotonicity (equivalently, its Puiseux behavior at
+infinity), $u_j(r)$ has a limit and $r u_j'(r)\to0$; thus
+$\gamma_j'(r)=u_j(r)+r u_j'(r)$ has norm tending to $1$.
+Using \eqref{eq:scalar-rabier},
+\[
+ |(F\circ\gamma_j)'(r)|
+ \le \lambda(\gamma_j(r))\|\gamma_j'(r)\|
+ \le 2r^{-(1+1/N)}.
+\]
+Therefore $F(\gamma_j(r))$ converges to a limit $c_j\in\C$, with
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:radial-tail}
+ |F(\gamma_j(r))-c_j|\le 2N r^{-1/N}.
+\end{equation}
+
+By Standard input~\ref{std:asymptotic}(ii), any
+$p\in L_{j,r}$ can be joined to $\gamma_j(r)$ inside $L_{j,r}$ by a curve
+of length at most $Mr$.  Along that curve
+$\|\dd F\|=\lambda\le r^{-(1+1/N)}$, so
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:spherical-diameter}
+ |F(p)-F(\gamma_j(r))|\le M r^{-1/N}.
+\end{equation}
+Combining \eqref{eq:radial-tail} and
+\eqref{eq:spherical-diameter}, the image of
+$E_j\cap\{\rho\ge R\}$ is contained in a ball about $c_j$ whose radius is
+$O(R^{-1/N})$.  Consequently every sequence in
+$\widetilde\Sigma_R$ escaping to infinity and having bounded $F$-value has,
+after passage to one of the finitely many components, limit $c_j$.
+Equation~\eqref{eq:Kinf-tail} gives
+\[
+ K_\infty(F)\subset\{c_1,\ldots,c_k\}.
+\]
+Thus $B$ is finite.
 \end{proof}
 
